-- title:
--   Naturality of the Fontaine functor in the test algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $\mathcal O$ a commutative ring equipped with an algebra map to $\mathbf Z/p$ whose kernel is the ideal generated by $p$; fix $r$ and a Honda system $H_1$ for the element $p$ on $\mathcal O^r$ (additive operators $F,V$ with $F\circ V=V\circ F=p$, together with the submodule $H_1.L$ and its three conditions), a tower $G_v$ of commutative Hopf algebras over $\mathbf Z/p$ with bialgebra transition maps $s_v\colon G_{v+1}\to G_v$, additive maps $\pi_v\colon\mathcal O^r\to \mathrm{DieudonneModule}(\mathbf Z/p,p,G_v)$, and a datum $\mathcal S$ of split coordinates for these data which is `Lawful`. Let $g,g'$ be commutative $\mathcal O$-algebras, each adically complete for the ideal generated by $p$, with $p$ a non-zero-divisor in $g'$, and let $\varphi\colon g\to g'$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra map. Let $z=(\xi,\eta)$, with $\xi\colon H_1.L\to g[1/p]$ $\mathcal O$-linear and $\eta\colon\mathcal O^r\to \mathrm{UnipotentWittCovector}\,p\,(\mathbf Z/p\otimes_{\mathcal O}g)$ additive, lie in `fontaineFunctor` for the inclusion $g\to\mathbf Z/p\otimes_{\mathcal O}g$: that is, $\eta$ carries $F$ to Frobenius and $V$ to Verschiebung, and for each $l\in H_1.L$ some unipotent Witt covector over $g$ maps to $\eta(l)$ and has $\mathrm{wUp}$ congruent to $\xi(l)$ modulo `PLoc.pSub`. Assume $z$ is described at some level $v$ by algebra maps $f\colon\mathcal S.Gc\,v\to\mathbf Z/p\otimes g$, $e\colon\mathcal S.Ge\,v\to\mathbf Z/p\otimes g$, an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $y\colon\mathcal S.Et\,v\to g$ and coordinates $x\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,\mathcal S.d\to g$ with every $x_j$ in the radical of $(p)$, in the sense that $\eta$ is the Dieudonné evaluation of $(f\otimes e)\circ\mathcal S.\Theta_v$ composed with $\pi_v$, that $e\circ\mathcal S.\theta^e_v=\mathrm{id}\otimes y$, that $f(\mathcal S.\kappa_v X_i)=1\otimes x_i$, and that $\xi(\mathcal S.\alpha_i)$ equals the sum of the two `wSeries` built from $n\mapsto\mathrm{adicEval}_{(p)}(x)(\mathcal S.a\,i\,n)$ and from $k\mapsto y(\mathcal S.\hat c\,i\,k\,v)$. Then the pair $(\mathrm{mapLinear}\,\varphi\circ\xi,\ \mathrm{UnipotentWittCovector.map}(\mathrm{id}\otimes\varphi)\circ\eta)$ lies in `fontaineFunctor` for $g'$; if $\eta$ annihilates $p^{v_0}\mathcal O^r$ for some $v_0$, so does its push-forward; and the push-forward is described at the same level $v$ by $(\mathrm{id}\otimes\varphi)\circ f$, $(\mathrm{id}\otimes\varphi)\circ e$, $\varphi\circ y$ and $\varphi\circ x$, the four describing identities holding verbatim with these data over $g'$.
--
--   This is the functoriality in the test algebra of Fontaine's functor of points attached to a Honda system, carried along with the connected–étale description of a point in terms of formal coordinates, an étale lift and the associated logarithmic series. It is used in the construction of a multivariable formal group cocycle from split coordinates, [`Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_mvFormalGroup_cocycle_of_splitCoordinates`](thm.html#Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_mvFormalGroup_cocycle_of_splitCoordinates).
